WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.790
Now, just to give you a nugget from my

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.390
own personal workflow, I use this skill

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.350
called calibrate, which basically at the

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.710
end of almost every session, I run this

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:10.750
skill because what it does is it just

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.830
looks over the session that you just had

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.990
with Claude Code, and it spots where you

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.550
corrected it or where you gave it

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.910
feedback throughout the chat as you talk

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.190
to it. And it folds those lessons into

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.230
its memory. It gives suggestions to the

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.070
skills that it needs to update. And to

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.710
me, that is very important because

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.270
naturally, as you converse with Claude

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:30.190
Code, you talk to it sort of like a

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:31.950
teammate, right? And what you want at

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.950
the end of the session or at the end of

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.710
that sort of coaching exercise with your

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.270
teammate is that they calibrate or learn

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.350
from their actions in that conversation

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:42.670
in order to improve their own practice

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:44.230
and way you're working. To give a quick

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.630
example of one of my own sessions, you

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.310
can see here that I invoked this skill

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.310
called recording, which just basically

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.390
goes through a YouTube video of mine,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.190
gets the transcript of it, and you can

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.110
see this is coming from one of our

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.190
Agents of Our Coffee live session in the

00:00:58.200 --> 00:00:59.990
community, and it gives timestamps

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.390
automatically for that hour-long

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.070
session, which just helps automate that

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.829
whole process. And at the end of this,

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:07.110
you can see that I used calibrate to

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:09.550
update this skill as well as the memory.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:10.990
And because my Claude Code already knows

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.470
what this keyword is about, it was able

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:13.910
to update the recording skill

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.870
immediately, apply the changes that I

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:18.230
want made, updated the memory as well,

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:20.030
so that the next time I converse with it

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.950
on this particular topic, it already has

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.990
that knowledge to do better next time.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.390
And so, this is how you build up your

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.030
Agentic Operating System, which

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:29.030
snowballs into something that is really

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.110
custom for your setup. You start with

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:33.190
initial skills, you build up your

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.390
memory, and then you just continuously

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.310
work with Claude Code, use a skill

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.230
called calibrate every time that you end

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.190
a session, and then more and more, it

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.870
becomes smarter and tuned to whatever it

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:46.030
is that you need. So, it's becoming more

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.390
of a teammate rather than just a chatbot

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.750
that you maybe used to with something

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:51.630
like ChatGPT. So, this skill, I'll

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:53.230
include it down in the resources below.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:54.910
So, I suggest for you to try it out and

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.630
also just personalize it. See if it

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.710
works for you or even tweak it so that

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.120
it fits your setup better.
